WEBVTT

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.810
KERIM SATIRLI: Hi, my
name is Kerim Satirli,

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:07.140
I'm a Senior Developer Advocate
at HashiCorp, we're focused

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.200
on infrastructure and
orchestration tooling.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.840
Thank you for joining our
session on how to discover,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.990
govern and scale
Microsoft Azure resources

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.350
with HashiCorp Terraform.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:23.760
Now, today's session is about a
problem every Azure team has,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.540
but very few have a clean answer
for, the gap between what's

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.240
in your Terraform state and
what's actually running

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.210
in your subscriptions
and tenants.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.490
So sit back and let's
get ready to dive in.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.500
Every practitioner and
customer team I talk

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:46.050
to gives me a coverage number,
80%, 90, sometimes higher.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.480
Now, when we actually sit down
and compare Terraform state

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.300
against what's running
in their subscriptions,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.080
the real number is somewhere
between 40 and 60%.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.960
Some do really well
and actually hit 80%,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.130
but no matter their number,
they all think one thing,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.820
we do everything with Terraform.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.230
The gap here isn't dishonesty,
it's that nobody's measuring.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.170
Resources accumulate outside
infrastructure's code

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:17.750
for entirely human reasons,
which we'll get to in a moment.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.730
And then the path
back from unmanaged

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.820
to managed has historically
required a heroic engineering

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.940
effort or fees you
couldn't actually afford.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.040
That's a problem
decisions about.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.600
By the end of the
next 20 minutes,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.680
you'll have seen a workflow that
closes that gap in a quarter,

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.770
rather than a quarter year.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.140
Quick sidebar, if you've heard
of infrastructures code,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.080
but aren't entirely sure
what it or Terraform is,

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.500
think of it this way,
the traditional way

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.640
to provision resources and
deploy resources in Azure is

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.460
to open the Azure portal,
find the service you want

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.210
and then manually click to
get at your infrastructure.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:02.820
While this seems fast,
it is error prone,

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.740
because to get the
same exact result,

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.420
you have to click the same
exact buttons next time

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.270
and not make any mistakes.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.910
Comparatively, infrastructures
code allows you

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:18.420
to describe your infrastructure
in human readable text file

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.550
that you conversion
control in review

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.890
with your colleagues easily.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.040
And because it is code, tools
like Terraform can parse it

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.330
and turn your pros into
provision infrastructure.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.070
So with all those benefits,
you might wonder,

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.130
how do resources end
up not being codified?

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:42.960
Well, there are four partners
I see most often and none

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.830
of them are actually failures
of engineering discipline.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.040
The first is the most common,
I'll codify it later.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.860
Somebody with all the right
intentions clicks something

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.710
in the portal during
exploration, fully meaning

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.950
to bring under management later.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.500
But later never arrives.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.310
The second is a 2 a.m. incident
fix, because writing Terraforms

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.530
and any infrastructures
code while production is

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.490
down is sometimes a hard
sell, especially for teams

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.520
that do not codify everything.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.700
The third is a classic,
the acquisition.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.070
You inherit an environment
you've never seen

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.730
and you don't even know
what's in it, all you know is

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.550
that there's a new subscription
in your account with stuff

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.910
that doesn't look
like it belongs.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.210
And the fourth is the POC
that became production,

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:37.230
because it worked, and nobody
had time to rebuild it properly,

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:41.140
because it was already live
and serving customers.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.300
Point of this list isn't to
prevent these situations,

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.840
you can't, you absolutely can't,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.270
they are going to
keep happening.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:54.940
The point is to have
a fast path back.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:00.690
And this is even worse when
you're running AI workloads.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.270
Not a genta clube [assumed
spelling] to generate code, no,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:06.870
actual GPU resources
for your AI LLM

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.000
and machine learning
infrastructure.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.890
When you can't attribute cost,

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.600
because you're missing
important tags,

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.610
your organization will have
a harder time figuring

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.980
out which experiment, which team

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.150
and which project a given
resource belongs to.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:24.450
And, of course, network
isolation is a huge problem.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:25.770
Your training environment

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.260
and your inference
environment have very,

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.390
very different security postures

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:34.200
and one ungoverned subnet
can collapse that boundary.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.990
Identity sprawl is a third
and this one's specific

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.030
to agent based architectures.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.950
Every agent has
managed identity,

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.990
every managed
identity has scopes

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.880
and if those scopes
aren't defined in code,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:49.620
your audit story falls apart
the moment somebody asks.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.620
And we're not even diving into
the blast radius of leaking data

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.180
because your storage
account is allowed

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.450
to be publicly accessible.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:00.450
Now, if we briefly switch our
terminal, Terraform, of course,

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:01.830
understands this problem.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.710
Traditionally, you'd use the
CLI to import some Terraform

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.470
with the import command.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:09.830
Terraform has support for
this and has supported it

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:11.930
for years and still does.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.140
It works, but is very
much one resource,

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:19.980
one address at a time and,
therefore, is time consuming.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.400
Since Terraform 1.5 E can
do this with import blocks.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.460
Discovery is still in U, but
this is a real improvement

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.440
over the old workflow.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.700
You get a plan first model, you
can review the generated config

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.140
in a pull request and you can
apply it when you're happy.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.660
If you've adopted this
pattern, you're already

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.810
in way better shape
than most teams.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.420
But notice what's still
missing, you're still writing

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.460
that import block yourself.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.020
Even if you have some
tooling that may infer it.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.610
This means that you already need

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:58.020
to know what the resource ID is
inside Azure, you need to know

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.890
that the resource
actually exists.

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:06.360
Now, in the Terraform search,
this feature is available

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.210
in the Terraform CLI
since Terraform 1.14,

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.090
which was released
in November 2025

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:15.570
and it upgrades the way
you're going to do imports

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:19.530
in the future in an
absolutely amazing way.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:24.270
The whole process is so simple,
it consists of three steps.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:28.410
First, we define a
search query, say,

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:32.250
give me all unmanaged MS SQL
servers in my subscription

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:35.130
that INSMD build resource group.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:39.300
Then you run the Terraform
query command on the CLI

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.280
and let Terraform do its job.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.340
It authenticates to Azure
the way you normally do

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.020
and starts querying
the remote API

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:48.570
for all the unmanaged servers.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:49.560
Give this a minute or two,

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.060
depending on how
much data you have

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.270
and how many resources
your query is looking at

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:58.920
and then you're ready for
step three, generating code.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.290
Yep, no more hand
coding everything,

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:05.910
hoping you don't make copy/paste
errors or forget an attribute,

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:10.680
with Terraform search, you get
the code generated for you,

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.310
store it in the.TFL, so you
can expect it, peer review it

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.800
and make sure you're
aligning your resources

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.930
to your organization's
requirements.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:26.340
Search, invert the
import question.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.370
Instead of a situation
where it's very much run,

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:32.610
I know what I want, now
help me write the config,

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:36.120
this changes it to,
show me what's there,

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.770
generate the config from it and
then let me plan the import.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:46.740
Three commands, instead of
three weeks of inventory work.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.300
Let that sink in for a second.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:52.600
And now let me show you.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.430
Let's first have a look at
what we're dealing with.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.220
I'll open the Azure
portal in my browser

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:04.440
and I can see our subscription,
I'm in a single resource group

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:08.670
and you can see roughly 30
resources scattered across it.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:13.200
Networking foundations,
security groups, a nat gateway,

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.770
a storage account and a
function app and, of course,

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:19.170
private DNS to put
it all together,

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:23.340
public IP addresses,
and much more.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.600
Sadly, all of this is unmanaged.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.970
And you're not even
seeing the worse of it.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:32.730
Some of these have
tags, others don't,

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:37.590
sometimes the tags match a
format, sometimes they don't.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:41.490
And, now, I know this is not a
heavy ask, but pretend for sake

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.950
of this demo that three
different teams click this

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:46.470
environment together over
the course of a year,

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.070
I know I've been there, I
know you've been there,

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:52.580
so you know what we're looking
at, different conventions,

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:56.840
no central inventory, nobody's
entirely sure what depends

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:01.550
on what and which
resources we can upgrade

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:03.990
without breaking the others.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:10.560
So, let's change that, let's
remove some of the risk

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.840
and make this useable.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.030
First, we're write out the query

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:19.410
for the resources we
want to discover.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:21.930
There are too many resources
to fit in a single screen,

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:23.820
so I'll highlight three of them.

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:25.440
I'll start with a nat gateway,

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:28.710
because I absolutely
want that to be managed.

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.720
Then the security groups,

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.500
because if there's
anything I want to manage

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:39.210
through Terraform right away, it
is definitely my security stuff.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:42.000
And, of course, the
two public addresses.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.910
By managing them through
Terraform, I can bring them

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.750
into other systems, I can use
the whole Terraform provider

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:51.630
ecosystem, and make
sure those IPs end

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.430
up in my monitoring tooling,

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:56.900
they end up in my
on-premise systems

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:01.980
that need access and much more.

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:04.170
Now, if you're looking at
your screen, you'll notice

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:08.160
that I'm not specifying any
Azure credentials in this file.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:13.020
Reason for that is simple,
Terraform search inherits these

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:15.030
from your overall
provider config,

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:19.260
so as long as your normal
Terraform plan run can succeed,

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:22.890
Terraform query, the
CLI command, can also

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.740
and will also succeed.

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:27.420
If I want to be even
more prescriptive,

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:30.450
I can configure each
query to look only

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:33.270
at a specific research
group, for example.

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:37.740
In this case I'm saying,
for the nat gateway,

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.140
only list unmanaged resources

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:44.340
in the Microsoft built 2026
unmanaged resource group,

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:47.620
which are created specifically
for this session.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:50.640
Then back to Terraform

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:53.970
and execute the
Terraform query command.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:57.450
And after a few seconds
and a few CPU cycles,

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:00.090
we'll see our resources appear.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.390
Now, for the sake of this demo,
I'm showing you the first two,

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:06.630
but anything I query
for, which will appear,

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:09.960
as long as there's a
match for my query.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.200
Keep in mind that if you
set up constraints here,

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:17.310
that result in the API
not returning anything,

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:20.040
Terraform also won't
be able to find it.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:23.460
So don't hard code the variable,

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:26.760
don't hard core the resource
names, use variables

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:29.370
and make sure
everything makes sense.

00:11:29.370 --> 00:11:31.620
But, in this case,
everything looks good,

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:33.930
so let's export our config.

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:37.620
For this, I'll use a Terraform
query command again,

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:42.690
but this time I specify the path
I want to save the config to.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:46.380
And, again, after a few CPU
cycles I can switch to my editor

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:51.310
and inspect the newly
generated unmanaged.TF file.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.480
And I'm showing an exert here,

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.870
because ultimately those four
resources generated about 2,

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:02.520
300 lines of import statements
and resource definitions,

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.010
but right from the
top you can see

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:06.600
that Terraform left a comment

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.840
to let you know this
is a generated file.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:11.580
Not shown on screen,
but definitely

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:14.940
in your file is a word of
warning from Terraform

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:18.390
to inspect and
verify everything,

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:20.100
we'll do that in the background

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:22.920
and meanwhile
continue on-boards.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:24.360
The four resources

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:29.100
that I discovered are good
for almost 300 lines.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.650
So I invite you to try
this out yourself instead

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:35.850
of watching me scroll
through a long file.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.880
But I do want to
show you one part.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:43.260
Below each discovered and
config generated resource,

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:44.970
you also have an import block

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:48.900
to easily import this
newly discovered,

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:52.530
and currently unmanaged resource
into your Terraform state.

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:56.760
All it takes is one Terraform
plan and apply cycle.

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:59.940
And, of course, this is
using the import block,

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:03.340
you can easily use HCP
Terraform for this.

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:08.040
Now at this point you might be
wondering, are we done now?

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:09.900
We imported everything, right?

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:14.010
No more unmatched resources,
we learned how to do this

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:17.040
in an almost programmatic
way, you can bring

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:21.630
in the HashiCorp Terraform agent
skills and get a lot of this set

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:27.300
up programmatically and through
agentic loops and, yes,

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:30.810
we could totally stop here, we
could take a few minutes back,

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.240
but there's one more
thing I want to show you.

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:38.700
We already made huge
leaps, now I want to go

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:42.480
from managed to the next level.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.630
We discovered we imported,
but in doing so,

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:49.080
we retained all the
old configurations.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:54.030
Terraform did not make any
changes to it, unless you did.

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:57.210
So, the managed section is done,

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.030
so let's mark this
section as done.

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:03.330
Now, what's next is to apply
our organizational policies

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:06.360
to our newly imported resources.

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:08.730
This will allow us
to eliminate drift

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:12.390
and make sure any resources
we imported feel and behave

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:15.480
like other resources,
with the right tag,

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:17.400
the right naming
conventions and many,

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:20.820
many more things we can
create it for, such as,

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:23.100
appropriate instance types,

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:26.580
making sure we're
using the right SKUs,

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:30.660
whatever you can imagine and
want to create a policy for,

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:34.560
this is a path you should be on.

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.150
So let's switch
back to our editor

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:38.430
and create a Sentinel policy.

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:42.840
HashiCorp Sentinel is a policy
framework that blocks Terraform

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.630
from carrying out operations
that are not in line

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:48.480
with your policy definitions.

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:50.310
We'll start by defining types

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.920
of resources we want
to match against.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:57.210
Here we've got our set of
public IPs, the nat gateway

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:59.550
and the security code.

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:03.090
Next, we'll collect
all planned resources

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:06.870
that are taggable
whenever we run a plan.

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:09.630
And then we'll process
these resources in a loop

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:11.850
and collect their tags.

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:14.880
You'll notice a comment sign
there, because in the interest

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:17.370
of not flooding your screen,

00:15:17.370 --> 00:15:20.580
I'm only showing you a few
lines of code at a time.

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:23.340
So let's look at the
policy separately.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:25.530
Here we've got a simple
conditional where we check

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:28.320
for violations, like,
a missing owner tag

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:31.500
or an owner tag in
the wrong format.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.480
Now this is purely
for demo purposes.

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:38.280
Of course, in your case, you'll
have many, many more policies

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:39.240
and if you're wondering,

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:41.370
how do I even get started
with that checkout?

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:45.600
The Terraform registry at
registry.terraform.io,

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:47.520
where we have a handful

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:51.540
of amazing policy
packs specifically

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:53.770
for your Azure use case.

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:58.200
But in this case, our
policy is written,

00:15:58.200 --> 00:15:59.460
so just like with Terraform,

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:03.180
we'll apply the policy
through Sentinel.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:05.520
And, again, after
a few CPU cycles,

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:09.810
we can see that the results
return and our resources are,

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:13.990
in fact, not aligned with the
organization's naming policy.

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:17.190
This is exactly
what we expected.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:20.790
We imported resources that
we knew were not following

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:23.730
organizational guidelines.

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:28.470
Terraform happily imports those,
Sentinel then prevents you

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:30.210
from making further changes

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:33.690
until you've
eliminated this drift.

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:37.170
That being said,
we're using Sentinel

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:40.740
in a soft-fail mode here,
so we could still continue

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:45.450
and just get an advisory,
but for what it's worth,

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:48.990
start working on a hard
fail as soon as possible

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:54.010
to make sure your infrastructure
is governed the right way.

00:16:55.890 --> 00:17:02.040
Though, fixing all of this
is a task for another time.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:04.560
For now, we're at the
stage where we can rely

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:07.800
on our infrastructure to be
Terraform managed and governed

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:11.170
by appropriate policies
using Sentinel.

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:15.900
And that brings us to the
conclusion of this session.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.000
Hopefully you've learned
something new and I've been able

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.100
to inspire you to
bring more resources

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:23.040
under Terraform management
and go the extra mile

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:26.940
to govern them appropriately
with organizational policies

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:30.190
that make sense for you
and your organization.

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:34.520
Remember, use the
Terraform query command

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:38.240
to bring unmanaged
resources under management

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.360
and use Sentinel to enforce
policies that make sense

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:43.790
for your organization.

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:48.030
Finally, here are three links
I think are worth looking at.

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:52.740
We've got the documentation
for the CLI commands,

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:57.270
a blog post diving into the why
and how of Terraform query and,

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:59.790
of course, a Sentinel playground

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:03.900
to see the full
policy in action.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:07.710
With that, thank you so much
